# Rest-iN-U Project Rules for AI Assistants

You are working on Rest-iN-U, a digital estate planning and legacy management platform.

## Project Structure
- `frontend/` - Next.js 14 app with TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui
- `backend/` - Express.js API with TypeScript, Prisma ORM
- `.claude/` - AI agent documentation and planning files
- `.github/workflows/` - CI/CD automation

## Code Style

### TypeScript
- Use strict TypeScript - no `any` types unless absolutely necessary
- Prefer interfaces over types for object shapes
- Use proper return type annotations on functions
- Use `const` by default, `let` only when reassignment is needed

### React/Next.js
- Use functional components with hooks
- Prefer named exports over default exports
- Use server components by default, client components only when needed
- Follow the app router conventions (app/ directory)
- Use shadcn/ui components from `@/components/ui/`

### File Naming
- React components: PascalCase (e.g., `UserProfile.tsx`)
- Utilities/hooks: camelCase (e.g., `useAuth.ts`, `formatDate.ts`)
- API routes: kebab-case directories (e.g., `api/user-profile/route.ts`)

### Import Order
1. React/Next.js imports
2. Third-party libraries
3. Internal components (`@/components/`)
4. Internal utilities (`@/lib/`, `@/hooks/`)
5. Types (`@/types/`)
6. Styles

## Commit Messages
Use conventional commits format:
- `feat:` - New feature
- `fix:` - Bug fix
- `docs:` - Documentation changes
- `style:` - Code style changes (formatting)
- `refactor:` - Code refactoring
- `test:` - Adding/updating tests
- `chore:` - Maintenance tasks

Example: `feat(estate): add beneficiary management component`

## Testing
- Write tests for new components and utilities
- Use Jest for unit tests, Playwright for E2E
- Test files go in `__tests__/` directories or alongside components

## API Design
- RESTful endpoints in backend
- Use proper HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE)
- Return consistent JSON responses with `success`, `data`, `error` fields
- Handle errors with appropriate status codes

## Security
- Never commit secrets or API keys
- Validate all user inputs
- Use parameterized queries (Prisma handles this)
- Sanitize data before rendering

## Performance
- Use Next.js Image component for images
- Implement proper loading states
- Use React.memo() sparingly and only when needed
- Lazy load heavy components

## Documentation
- Add JSDoc comments for complex functions
- Keep README files updated
- Document API endpoints in code comments

## When Making Changes
1. Read existing code patterns first
2. Follow established conventions in the codebase
3. Run `npm run lint` before committing
4. Ensure TypeScript has no errors
5. Add tests for new functionality

## Agent System
This project uses AI agents defined in `.claude/`. When activating an agent:
- Read the agent file: `.claude/3-agents/[AGENT_CODE].md`
- Follow the agent's specific instructions and constraints
- Maintain context across the conversation
